Title: blackbox: stderr routing audit and pin
Created: 2026-05-30
Author: MinhHaDuong

--- log ---
2026-05-30T07:04Z MinhHaDuong created

--- body ---
## Context

A script that pipes `erg list | jq` or captures `erg new` output breaks silently if an error
message leaks to stdout. The convention is: success output to stdout, all diagnostics to stderr.

A source audit of `src/go/*.go` shows all `fmt.Fprintf(os.Stderr, ...)` calls look correct. But
the audit was done by grep — it does not verify that every *error return path* actually reaches
one of those calls. And there is no blackbox pin: removing `os.Stderr` from a `fmt.Fprintf` call
would not be caught by any existing test.

## Part 1 — Source audit findings (from imagine phase)

Two confirmed bugs — failure output goes to stdout, not stderr:

- **`check.go` lines 200-206**: `ERG CHECK FAILED`, per-violation `VIOLATION` lines, and
  warning lines all use `fmt.Printf` → stdout. Must move to `fmt.Fprintf(os.Stderr, ...)`.
- **`validate.go` lines 309-311**: `ERG VALIDATION FAILED` and per-violation lines use
  `fmt.Printf` → stdout. Must move to `fmt.Fprintf(os.Stderr, ...)`.

All other commands route correctly (close, log, rm, new, archive, migrate, tag, list, update).
`validate`'s "No .erg files found" (line 300) is a success-path exit-0 stdout write — correct.

## Part 2 — Fix

Move the failure-output `fmt.Printf` calls in `check.go` and `validate.go` to `os.Stderr`.
Keep all success-path output on stdout.

## Part 3 — Blackbox pin (`test_stderr.sh`)

New `tests/test_stderr.sh`. For each command, trigger an error and assert stdout is empty:

- `erg close NONEXISTENT reason DIR 2>/dev/null` → stdout empty
- `erg log NONEXISTENT "line" DIR 2>/dev/null` → stdout empty
- `erg rm NONEXISTENT DIR 2>/dev/null` → stdout empty
- `erg new "" DIR 2>/dev/null` → stdout empty
- `erg check DIR 2>/dev/null` (with a corpus containing a duplicate ID) → stdout empty
- `erg validate BADFILE 2>/dev/null` (with a malformed file) → stdout empty

Do not add negative controls in this file — existing per-command tests already cover valid-input
output. The negative controls are YAGNI here.

Use a new `test_stderr.sh`, not `test_main.sh` (which covers only top-level dispatch).

## Exit criteria

- [ ] `check.go` failure output moved to stderr
- [ ] `validate.go` failure output moved to stderr
- [ ] `tests/test_stderr.sh` added with the six error-trigger checks above
- [ ] `make check` passes
